WEBVTT

NOTE
Automatic English captions. Generated locally and checked with additional audio decodes; not fully audited by listening.

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.360
Loops are one of the most fundamental
ideas in programming, a way to repeat the

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.460
same step across a whole set of items. Sim
gives you two blocks for this, the loop

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:15.520
block and the parallel block. They do the
same job, running whatever's inside once

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:20.980
for every item in a collection, but they
go about it in very different ways. Let me

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:25.980
walk you through how each one works and
when to reach for one over the other. Some

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:30.360
jobs are a list. The same steps need to
run for every item. For that, Sim has the

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.640
loop, a block that holds other blocks.
Whatever you place inside it runs once per

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.660
item. The loop is configured with a
collection to iterate over. Three items

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.440
here, so everything inside will run three
times.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.500
Watch the current item reference. On the
first pass, it resolves to X, the same

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:53.240
steps run again with Y and then Z. A loop
takes the items strictly in order.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:02.700
After the last pass, the loop exits once.
Downstream, the loop results value is an

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:08.020
array holding every pass's output in
order. Now swap the container to parallel.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:12.900
The steps inside don't change. Only the
schedule does. References follow the name

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:18.360
so the tags update on their own. A
parallel block runs one copy of the inside

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:23.200
for every item, all at the same time. They
finish together and the same results array

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:24.200
comes out.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:32.040
One shape, two schedules. Use a loop when
the order matters and parallel when it

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.040
doesn't.
